# Record

- **Record**: The set of writer logs a node holds. Every derived artifact
  derives from it and the node's own key set (I10); the audit log, counters and
  **configuration** sit beside it: the principal's settings (§9.6). A change to
  configuration takes effect only once the widening act accepting it records
  its digest, except a change that only cuts; that acceptance adds, edits or
  unpins its configuration pins (ADM-04). A repository's `.parinn.toml`, its
  **repository configuration**, declares no pins and may make only changes a
  cut act could make, never removing a pin from a restore block (ADM-04). Managed policy, configuration and repository configuration are the
  three **settings layers**. A settings layer's cut-only change only narrows
  settings; quarantine and key revocation happen only as recorded principal
  acts. The **store** is the home's files containing the
  record, its derived artifacts and payloads (§8.1).
- **Writer**: One seat's append-only log, written on one node⟦f:paired-phone⟧ or paired phone⟦/f⟧
  and held by any node, named by its **writer id**, derived from the seat's
  first key. Its events are hash-chained; the **chain head** at a seq is the
  hash over every event up to it.
⟦f:segment-exchange⟧- **Retire a writer**: Mark a writer retired: by a principal act⟦f:access-tokens⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧, or by the
  expire act ending the access token whose token key certified its seat key⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧.
  The retirement names the writer's last accepted seq, and segments past it are
  marked delivered after retirement.⟦f:peer-sync⟧ A peer still accepts those that continue
  its chain without a fork; only a revocation refuses them (PEER-05).⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧
- **Event**: One immutable entry in a writer, such as a message (the harness's
  user input or the model's reply), tool call, tool result, **hook observation**
  (what a hook reported)⟦f:git-tracking⟧, worktree checkpoint⟦/f⟧⟦f:launcher⟧⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:git-tracking⟧, the launcher's event about a room's
  branch⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧, key rotation⟦f:purge-retention⟧, tombstone⟦/f⟧, or an
  act (a room act, a principal act⟦f:principal-keys⟧ or an expire act⟦/f⟧), a **recall event** (one
  recall, RCL-07), a canary event⟦f:store-protection⟧, a backup event⟦/f⟧⟦f:pin-candidates⟧, an agent's pin candidate⟦/f⟧⟦f:segment-exchange⟧, a⟦f:purge-retention⟧n
  erasure or⟦/f⟧ quarantine request⟦/f⟧, or an unparsed line. Its **event kind** is
  which kind of entry it is, recorded apart from its provenance.
- **seq**: An event's position in its writer: strictly increasing, gap-free and
  never reused (REC-06).
- **Address**: (writer, seq), shown as short `A2·4812` (its writer label and
  seq), range `A2·4812–5025` or full `parinn:room/<room>/w/<writer>/<seq>`. Every
  shown form is accepted wherever an address is taken, and so is one ASCII input
  form, `<label>:<seq>` or `<label>:<from>-<to>` (for example `A2:12`,
  `A2:30-40`), with the writer id accepted in place of the label (RCL-08). A
  **writer label** is a writer's short display alias, unique per node, each
  **writer-label assignment** recorded as a structural event in the assigning
  device seat's personal-room writer. An address range lies in one writer;
  "range" on its own means an address range. The only meaning of "address".
- **Payload**: The full content of a large event, stored outside the event in
  the store's **payload store**, under a name that confirms no guess at its
  content (REC-09).
- **Segment**: A range of one writer, sealed as a unit when it closes⟦f:peer-sync⟧; what
  peers ⟦f:git-carrier⟧and the git carrier ⟦/f⟧exchange⟦/f⟧. A unit of the record, not a storage
  layout, though a writer's closed segments may be merged (REC-25)⟦f:purge-retention⟧ or rewritten
  by a purge (ADM-07)⟦/f⟧ without changing any address. The **open segment** is a
  writer's newest, still growing; it closes at the points REC-19 names⟦f:peer-sync⟧, and
  peers exchange its sealed prefix⟦/f⟧.⟦f:segment-exchange⟧ A closed segment or an open segment's sealed
  prefix is a **sealed range**.⟦/f⟧
- **Seal**: A seat key's signature over its writer id, a seq and the chain head
  at that seq (REC-18), made where that key lives: a witnessed run's run seat's,
  but the seat ingest starts, by its run's MCP server once it holds the key,
  else by the core (Run seat), covering what the hook handlers appended, an ingested run's by the core⟦f:paired-phone⟧, a paired phone's device
  seat's by the phone (OQ-39)⟦/f⟧, and every other writer's by the core⟦f:launcher⟧⟦f:browser-room-view⟧⟦f:bridges⟧; how the
  core signs and seals what the room-view component, the launcher and the bridge
  component record rests on OQ-40⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧. Events after the newest seal are unsigned.
- **Commitment**: A keyed commitment to an event's content under a per-event
  random key, its **commitment key**, kept with the content⟦f:purge-retention⟧ and erased with it⟦/f⟧;
  the only way the chain and seals⟦f:purge-retention⟧, and tombstones,⟦/f⟧ refer to content (REC-17).
- **Provenance**: An event's class from a closed set, saying what produced its
  content (PRV-01): `user`, `assistant`, `tool_call`, `tool_result:<tool>`,
  `web`, `mcp:<server>`, `file`, `subagent_result`, `harness_meta`,
  `harness_text`, `operator`⟦f:conversation⟧, `post`⟦/f⟧⟦f:facilitator⟧, `summary`⟦/f⟧, `structural` and `unparsed`.
  `harness_meta` is what the harness reports⟦f:launcher⟧, or the launcher records,⟦/f⟧ about the
  harness's operation, never free text: ⟦f:launcher⟧turn triggers, ⟦/f⟧model tokens, metadata
  lines that carry no free text⟦f:sandbox-risk⟧ and sandbox state⟦/f⟧ (PRV-08⟦f:sandbox-risk⟧, OWN-22⟦/f⟧); `operator`
  is the class of principal acts⟦f:principal-keys⟧, expire acts⟦/f⟧ and device-seat pins; ⟦f:conversation⟧posts are
  `post` and ⟦/f⟧run-seat pins `assistant`; every other room act⟦f:facilitator⟧ but a room summary⟦/f⟧
  takes its seat's **pin class**, `operator` for a device seat and `assistant`
  for a run seat; ⟦f:facilitator⟧a room summary is `summary`, never trusted; ⟦/f⟧⟦f:segment-exchange⟧a⟦f:purge-retention⟧n erasure or⟦/f⟧
  quarantine request a principal act sends is `operator`⟦f:purge-retention⟧, an erasure request a
  retention policy sends `structural`⟦/f⟧; ⟦/f⟧hook observations, key rotations⟦f:purge-retention⟧,
  tombstones⟦/f⟧⟦f:store-protection⟧, backup events⟦/f⟧⟦f:witness-check⟧⟦f:results-and-verdicts⟧, a witness check's record (the command by commitment, its exit
  status and the tree hash, OWN-18)⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:launcher⟧⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:git-tracking⟧, the launcher's events about a room's branch⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧ and canary events are `structural`; a recall event is
  `tool_call`⟦f:git-tracking⟧, and a worktree checkpoint `file`, its commit and branch being
  structural fields⟦/f⟧.
- **Origin**: How an event reached this node's record: `witnessed` (recorded
  live on this node: by its hook handlers, its CLI, TUI, MCP server⟦f:launcher⟧, launcher⟦/f⟧⟦f:browser-room-view⟧,
  room-view component⟦/f⟧⟦f:bridges⟧ or bridge component⟦/f⟧) or `ingested` (appended by `parinn
  ingest`, whatever transcript it reads, past an ingest marker included)⟦f:bundle-import⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧,
  or `bundle` (read by import)⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:segment-exchange⟧, or `peer` (received ⟦f:peer-sync⟧from a peer⟦f:paired-phone⟧, a paired phone⟦/f⟧⟦f:git-carrier⟧ or
  ⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:git-carrier⟧through the git carrier⟦/f⟧)⟦/f⟧ (RCL-09); the trust policy reads only whether an
  event was recorded on this node or ingested⟦f:segment-exchange⟧, or received (`peer`)⟦/f⟧⟦f:bundle-import⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧, or read from a
  bundle (`bundle`), which it reads as another node's⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:bundle-import⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧⟦f:segment-exchange⟧, and `bundle` beside
  `peer` is a display mark⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧. A node clone's carried-over writers keep the
  origin they were recorded with, `witnessed` there meaning recorded live on the
  node it was cloned from; the trust policy reads them as another node's but for
  what the predecessors of its own device seats, however far back, wrote, once
  its lineage acceptance stands (Node, Trusted sources). A **witnessed run** is one this node's hook handlers
  watched. Independent of provenance. Each event also records its **recorder**,
  the part that recorded it (the hook handlers, the CLI, the TUI, the MCP
  server⟦f:launcher⟧, the launcher⟦/f⟧⟦f:browser-room-view⟧, the room-view component⟦/f⟧⟦f:bridges⟧, or the bridge component, whose
  events take provenance `web`, always untrusted⟦/f⟧⟦f:browser-room-view⟧; the room-view component
  records a principal act taken in the browser room view, marked with its
  principal surface, and how it is signed rests on OQ-40⟦/f⟧), which the trust
  policy reads⟦f:peer-sync⟧; an event from a peer⟦f:bundle-import⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧ or a bundle⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧ records no recorder, only its
  origin⟦/f⟧.
⟦f:landmarks⟧- **Span**: A contiguous range of one run's events in one writer. A new span
  starts at every user turn, compaction, subagent start or end, and whenever the
  run's events move to another seat's writer (LMK-01).⟦/f⟧
⟦f:landmarks⟧- **Landmark**: A structural headline over one span, bound to its address range
  (LMK-02). LMK-06 may add a **natural-language headline** to a span whose every
  event is trusted, made only by a deterministic template over trusted fields;
  it is no structural field and never reaches a restore block. Landmarks roll up
  into **tiers** of at most `k` **landmark blocks**, each a sequence of
  landmarks; a full tier collapses its older landmark blocks to one line each,
  listing one address range per writer, into the next tier (LMK-05).⟦/f⟧
- **Structural field**: A field ParleyInn derives from an event's shape, never from
  its text: ids, kinds, counts, tool names, key fingerprints, addresses, version
  numbers, sanitized paths and exit status, sanitized under LMK-03. Sanitized,
  it is trusted whatever the event's trust level (PRV-06, LMK-03).
- **Structural event**: An event of provenance `structural`, whose meaning lies
  only in structural fields. A hook observation keeps only a hook's structural
  fields; content a hook carries is recorded under its own class. An act's
  structural fields count as structural fields, never the text it carries; the
  act keeps its own class.
- **Capture**: This node's hook handlers recording its runs' events; turning it
  off or pausing it is widening (OWN-11), and pausing turns it off until the
  principal turns it on again. What a run does while it is off is a capture gap
  (I1): once it is back on, the hook handlers' ingest position moves past what
  the run wrote meanwhile.
- **Ingest marker**: What a hook handler leaves when its hook budget cuts it
  short, so the next hook handler or `parinn ingest` resumes it (NFR-02).
  **Ingest** is reading a transcript into the record, by the hook handlers
  incrementally (REC-13) or by `parinn ingest`; what `parinn ingest` appends takes
  origin `ingested` and is never recorded by the hook handlers, even past an
  ingest marker. A transcript source's **ingest position** is how far ingest has
  read it: a byte offset and a hash of the consumed prefix (REC-03).
⟦f:git-tracking⟧- **Worktree checkpoint**: An event recording a worktree's commit, branch and
  redacted diff since the previous worktree checkpoint (REC-20).⟦/f⟧
- **Derived artifact**: Anything computed from the record and the node's key
  set, such as those I10 lists: the **search index** over event text among its
  indexes⟦f:needs-you⟧, the node's principal's Needs you queue among its queues⟦/f⟧, room state,
  trust levels, and run and integrity statuses⟦f:results-and-verdicts⟧. Results with their evidence
  classes are derived artifacts⟦/f⟧; ⟦f:room-status⟧room status, ⟦/f⟧⟦f:results-and-verdicts⟧a check's state and ⟦/f⟧a run status's
  time-relative freshness marks are computed where shown; the **quarantine set**
  is what a node holds quarantined.
- **Redaction**: Removing secrets from content before it is stored⟦f:bundle-import⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧, on import⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧,
  or from what an export's⟦f:publishing⟧, a publish's⟦/f⟧⟦f:shared-rooms⟧ or an invite's⟦/f⟧ review step sends⟦/f⟧,
  recorded (I1, SEC-08).
⟦f:purge-retention⟧- **Retention policy**: A rule (`retention_policy.*`), set by the node's
  principal or managed policy, that purges content per room and provenance class
  after a time (I1, SEC-22); the principal's change to one is widening and
  applies only once its configuration is accepted (ADM-04).⟦/f⟧
- **Flag**: A mark ParleyInn sets on an event whose text matches an injection
  pattern (PRV-07), shown with the event on Runs and inside the envelope of any
  recall that returns it⟦f:landmarks⟧; flagged text contributes only counts to landmarks (LMK-04)⟦/f⟧.
- **Quarantine**: Recorded, reversible exclusion of an event⟦f:landmarks⟧, span⟦/f⟧, run, writer
  or derived artifact from recall and restore blocks⟦f:landmarks⟧, and landmark text (LMK-04
  keeps its counts)⟦/f⟧, on the node that records it, without deletion (I5).
⟦f:purge-retention⟧- **Purge**: Deletion of content, appending a **tombstone** naming the purged
  range, which views show in its place (ADM-07): by a principal act, or by the
  node under a retention policy, recorded naming the policy. The only way stored
  content is destroyed (I1).⟦/f⟧
- **Gap marker**: What stands where content is missing: ⟦f:purge-retention⟧a tombstone for a purged
  range, ⟦/f⟧a **quarantine marker** for a quarantined address⟦f:compute-kernel⟧, or a **truncation
  marker** on capped kernel output⟦/f⟧.⟦f:segment-exchange⟧ A **missing range** is part of a writer this
  node does not hold⟦f:peer-sync⟧, such as a peer's lost tail⟦/f⟧.⟦/f⟧ A **capture gap** is part of a
  run its capture did not record.
- **Integrity status**: What a room or writer shows about its chain and seals,
  one of §9.7.5's values (VIEW-10). A refused segment is recorded as a
  structural event in this node's device seat's personal-room writer once per
  writer and reason, when first refused, beside its audit entry, so `refused`
  derives from the record and a rebuild appends nothing (REC-21, I10). The UI
  never says "secure".
⟦f:store-protection⟧- **Receipt**: A signed statement about a node's record, made to be kept apart
  from the home and checked with no network. Always qualified: a **head
  receipt** lists every writer's chain head at a moment, the tamper evidence of
  VIEW-10 and SEC-27⟦f:purge-retention⟧; a **purge receipt** states what a purge erased and what it
  could not (SEC-31)⟦/f⟧.⟦/f⟧
⟦f:store-protection⟧- **Backup**: A copy of a home's store and audit log, with no seat, device⟦f:access-tokens⟧,
  token⟦/f⟧ or at-rest key (ADM-06, SEC-10) (`parinn backup create`, ADM-06); reading
  it back is a backup restore. A backup restore takes a seat as a predecessor
  only once the seat's writer verifies, up to its newest seal, against a head
  receipt the principal kept apart; events past that seal, and every event of a
  writer that does not verify, are read as another node's.⟦/f⟧
⟦f:store-protection⟧- **At-rest key**: The key that encrypts the store when encryption at rest is on
  (SEC-09).⟦/f⟧
⟦f:export-bundles⟧- **Bundle**: A reviewed export of a room, signed by the device key of the
  device that exported it, its **exporter**⟦f:principal-keys⟧, which chains to the **bundle's
  principal key** once PRV-10 ships, the exporter's device key standing for it
  until then⟦/f⟧, carried as a file or a git ref.⟦/f⟧
⟦f:export-bundles⟧- **Trusted-only export**: The JSONL of the events trusted for the principal's
  agents, without the free text of cut or neutral principal acts, with full
  provenance, that downstream memory systems read (ADM-12,
  MEM-02); an export like a bundle (SEC-26).⟦/f⟧
⟦f:export-bundles⟧- **Export**: Writing a bundle, a rendering or a trusted-only export (`parinn
  export`), a widening principal act under SEC-26. Its review step shows the
  acting principal, before it takes effect, what will leave, SEC-08 applied.⟦f:publishing⟧ To **publish** is to serve a
  room's bundle read-only through the publish component⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:segment-exchange⟧, only as the room's
  visibility allows⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧; a widening principal act under SEC-26, and stopping it is
  cut.⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧
⟦f:export-bundles⟧- **Rendering**: A room rendered for people to read, with no keys or commitments
  (`parinn export --rendering`, SEC-26).⟦/f⟧
⟦f:bundle-import⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧- **Import**: Reading a bundle into this node's record (`parinn import`, REC-23).⟦f:peer-sync⟧
  A transcript is ingested and a peer's segments are received; neither is
  imported.⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧
⟦f:compute-kernel⟧- **Kernel**: The hermetic compute environment in which an agent runs code over
  the record (CMP-01); each run's **kernel variables** live in its own
  namespace.⟦/f⟧
